// ============================================================================
// keybindings.json — shared key bindings for Visual Studio Code and Cursor
// ============================================================================
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS
//   Custom key bindings, overriding the defaults. Like settings.json, this one
//   file serves both editors through symlinks — see that file's header for the
//   four destinations and the rules that apply to editing it.
//
// WHY IT IS EMPTY
//   Both editors' default bindings are good, and nothing here is currently
//   worth overriding. The file exists so that the moment you want a binding it
//   is already version-controlled, rather than being created outside the repo
//   and then needing to be adopted.
//
//   One binding was deliberately NOT carried over: Cursor had `cmd+i` bound to
//   `composerMode.agent`. It is a Cursor-only command, so it would be dead in
//   VS Code, and Cursor already binds its agent to a default shortcut.
//
// HOW TO ADD ONE
//   Objects of {"key", "command"}, optionally with "when" and "args":
//       { "key": "cmd+k cmd+t", "command": "workbench.action.selectTheme" }
//   Open the Keyboard Shortcuts UI (cmd+K cmd+S) to find a command's id, then
//   write it here rather than letting the UI write it — the UI edits this file
//   directly and its formatting will not match.
//
//   Bindings are platform-specific in VS Code's own sync model but not in this
//   file: use `cmd+` for macOS and `ctrl+` for Linux, or bind both.
// ============================================================================
[]
